::: {.doc-subject}

Add a barbell graph to the current graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L9240-L9382){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.add_barbell_graph(
    m1,
    m2,
    type=None,
    label=True,
    rel=None,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Barbell graph](../assets/graphs/add-barbell-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Three barbell graphs side by side: two triangles joined by one edge for m1=3 and m2=0, two triangles joined through a path of two nodes for m2=2, and two complete graphs on four nodes joined through one path node for m1=4 and m2=1. The cliques are the bells and the path is the bar." width="100%"}

Creates two complete graphs ("bells") of `m1` nodes each, joined by a path ("bar") of
`m2` nodes. The graph has `2 * m1 + m2` nodes, and with `m2=0` the two bells are joined
by a single edge. Every node on the bar, and every edge on it, is a cut point: removing
it splits the graph in two.

Nodes are created as the first bell, then the bar, then the second bell. The bar runs
from the last node of the first bell to the first node of the second, so the
numbering follows the shape from left to right; in a directed graph every edge points
from the earlier-created node to the later one. The graph is appended without touching
or connecting to existing nodes, and the whole construction is recorded as a single
step.

Barbell graphs are a standard stress test:

- community detection: two obvious clusters joined by a thin bridge
- random walks and diffusion: the bar is a bottleneck, so mixing and cover times are
  slow
- betweenness and bridge detection: all shortest paths between the bells use the bar
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[m1]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Number of nodes in each bell. Must be at least `2`.

<code>[m2]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Number of nodes on the bar between the bells. Must be non-negative; `m2=0` joins the
    bells with one edge.

<code>[type]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional value stored in the `type` attribute of every new node. If `None` (default),
    no `type` attribute is set.

<code>[label]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   If `True` (default), each new node is labelled with its node ID as a string; a label
    that is already used raises `ValueError` before anything is added. If `False`, the
    nodes are left unlabelled.

<code>[rel]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional value stored in the `rel` attribute of every new edge. If `None` (default),
    no `rel` attribute is set.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Self for method chaining. The graph is modified in place.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `m1=` is less than `2` or `m2=` is negative.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~add_lollipop_graph`) 

:   Add a clique with a path hanging from it.

[](`~articulation_points`) 

:   Find the nodes whose removal disconnects the graph.

[](`~bridges`) 

:   Find the edges whose removal disconnects the graph.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

A barbell graph is two complete graphs of `m1` nodes, the bells, joined by a path of `m2`
nodes, the bar. Two bells of four and a bar of two give `10` nodes: each bell has `6`
edges, and the bar adds `3` more:

::: {#028e64ce .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph().add_barbell_graph(m1=4, m2=2)

graph.count_nodes(), graph.count_edges()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(10, 15)
```
:::
:::


Every shortest path from one bell to the other runs along the bar, so the bar nodes, `"5"`
and `"6"`, have the highest betweenness, just ahead of the bell nodes they hang from, `"4"`
and `"7"`. The other bell nodes, such as `"3"`, are on no shortest path between others:

::: {#111916ea .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
scores = graph.betweenness_centrality(return_dict=True)
labels = ["3", "4", "5", "6", "7"]

{x: round(scores[graph.get_id_from_label(x)], 2) for x in labels}
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
{'3': 0.0, '4': 0.5, '5': 0.56, '6': 0.56, '7': 0.5}
```
:::
:::


### A bridge or a bar

With `m2=0` on the left, the bells are joined by a single bridge, and its two ends are the
only cut nodes, the nodes whose removal splits the graph. With a bar of two on the right,
every node on the way from one bell to the other is a cut node, and every edge along it a
bridge:

::: {#63584a23 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
bridge = ec.Graph().add_barbell_graph(m1=4, m2=0)

(
    len(bridge.articulation_points()),
    len(graph.articulation_points()),
    len(graph.bridges()),
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
```
(2, 4, 3)
```
:::
:::


::: {layout-ncol="2"}

::: {#8e5c1c0b .cell execution_count=4}
``` {.python .cell-code}
bridge.layout_kamada_kawai().plot(
    layout="pos", node_size=10, highlight=bridge.articulation_points(),
    label_by="label", width=320, height=320, legend=False,
)
```

